# Static and dynamic libraries

Compiled code reaches a running program in one of two ways: it is put
into the program's file when the program is built, or it is read from a
file of its own when the program runs. Rust does the first by default. A
package is the second.

## Static linking

A Rust program is made of crates. Cargo compiles each crate once into an
**`rlib`**, an archive of compiled code, and the **linker** then copies
what the program uses out of every `rlib` into one executable file.

```text
hql (the crate)   ─┐
regex             ─┤
serde_json        ─┼─ linker ─→  hql   one file, nothing else needed
…every other crate ─┘
```

This is **static linking**. The `hql` binary holds its own code and the
code of every crate it depends on. The builtin packages are part of it:
the extension of `std` is Rust inside the `hql` crate, linked in like the
rest. Static linking is why `hql` installs as a single file.

It is also why a builtin package cannot be added to. The set of crates is
fixed when the binary is built. Adding `ml` to `std` would mean building
`hql` again with `ml`'s crates in it, for everyone, whether they use it or
not.

## A dynamic library

A **dynamic library** is compiled code in a file of its own, in a format
the operating system can load into a process that is already running:
`.dylib` on macOS, `.so` on Linux, `.dll` on Windows. The loading program
names a file, and the system maps it into the program's memory. The
program then asks for a function in it by name, a **symbol**, and gets
back an address it can call.

In a crate's `Cargo.toml`, `crate-type = ["cdylib"]` asks the compiler for
this kind of file:

```sh
cargo build --release
ls -lh target/release/libhql_ext_hello.dylib
nm -gU target/release/libhql_ext_hello.dylib
```

```text
-rwxr-xr-x  754K  libhql_ext_hello.dylib
0000000000002ca0 T _hql_extension_v1
```

`nm -gU` lists the symbols a library exports. This one exports a single
function, `hql_extension_v1`. Every other function of the crate is inside
the file and has no exported name.

## A library carries its own copy of `hql`

The `hello` crate depends on the `hql` crate. A `cdylib` is linked the way
an executable is: the linker copies in what the crate uses from every
crate it depends on. So the library holds its own compiled copy of the
parts of `hql` it uses: `Value`, `Diagnostic`, the code that builds a
record. That is most of its 754K; the package's own functions are a few
hundred lines.

```text
the hql process
├── hql                          the binary
│   ├── the hql crate
│   ├── std's extension
│   └── the running program
└── libhql_ext_hello.dylib       the library
    ├── the hql crate            a second compilation of the same source
    ├── hello's five functions
    └── hql_extension_v1
```

The library is not linked against the `hql` binary, and it does not call
into it by name. The two files are two separate compilations of the same
source that happen to be in one process. Three things follow, and the rest
of the chapter is their consequences.

**The two copies must be the same compilation.** A `Value` built in the
library is read by the binary. Both must agree on what a `Value` is, byte
for byte ([[the-version-rule|the version rule]]).

**Each copy has its own state.** A global variable of the `hql` crate
exists twice. The running program's is the binary's. A native therefore
reaches the program through the context it is handed and through nothing
else ([[34-writing-an-extension/lambdas-and-context#why-everything-goes-through-the-context|chapter 34]]).

**The library is never unloaded.** The binary keeps pointers to the
library's functions and tables for as long as it runs, so the library
stays in memory until the process ends.

## What a library is not

| | A package's library | Another process | A WebAssembly module |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Runs in | the `hql` process | a process of its own | a sandbox inside the process |
| A call costs | a function call | a message each way | a boundary crossing |
| May do | anything `hql` may: files, network, memory | what its own process may | what the host grants |
| A crash | ends `hql` | ends the other process | is contained |

A package's library is native code with the rights of the `hql` process.
That is what makes it fast and unrestricted, and it is why a package is
installed deliberately, from a source you chose
([[37-the-registry-and-publishing|chapter 37]]).
